// Original specialist courses. Generated by scripts/build-specialist-courses.py.
const SPECIALIST_COURSE=[{"id": "specialist9-1", "core": 9, "domain": 1, "objective": "1.1", "title": "Scope an Azure access and governance plan", "type": "software", "requires": ["safety"], "lore": "Azure RBAC assigns a role to a security principal at a scope. Management group, subscription, resource group, and resource scopes form an inheritance hierarchy. Start with the task and smallest useful scope rather than assigning Owner to make an error disappear. Contributor can manage resources but cannot generally assign Azure RBAC roles. Reader can inspect resources without changing them. A management role does not automatically grant every data-plane permission; reading a storage account configuration differs from reading its blobs.", "flow": ["Identify task", "Choose principal and scope", "Inspect roles and policy", "Test allowed and denied actions"], "example": "A Contributor can create a VM in one location but receives a policy denial in another. RBAC permits the action; a separate location rule constrains the resource. Use an allowed location or obtain an approved policy exception, rather than granting Owner.", "sources": [{"title": "Azure Administrator official certification guide", "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/credentials/certifications/resources/study-guides/az-104"}], "lab": ["Draw a subscription with dev and production resource groups.", "Assign a fictional developer resource-management access only to dev; identify who may assign roles.", "Add a policy restricting locations and predict a denied deployment despite valid RBAC.", "Record two positive and two negative authorization tests."], "questions": [{"prompt": "An Azure developer should manage resources only in a dev resource group. Which assignment fits?", "options": ["Contributor at the dev resource group", "Owner at the subscription", "Reader at the tenant", "Contributor at every management group"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Contributor at the narrow scope supports resource management without broad assignment privileges.", "tier": 1, "id": "specialist9-1:q1"}, {"prompt": "Which Azure control can deny deployment in an unapproved location?", "options": ["A Reader role", "A DNS record", "A resource tag alone", "Azure Policy"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Policy can evaluate configuration such as location and deny noncompliant requests.", "tier": 1, "id": "specialist9-1:q2"}, {"prompt": "Why might Azure Reader access to a storage account not allow downloading a blob?", "options": ["The account must be public", "Tags override identity permissions", "Management access and blob data access are distinct", "Reader always downloads all data"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "A data-plane role or another authorized access method may be required.", "tier": 2, "id": "specialist9-1:q3"}, {"prompt": "A Contributor receives a location-policy denial. What is the targeted response?", "options": ["Create a new user with the same role", "Select an allowed location or seek an approved exception", "Assign Owner to bypass every policy", "Remove all subscription controls"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "A broader role does not remove a policy denial.", "tier": 2, "id": "specialist9-1:q4"}]}, {"id": "specialist9-2", "core": 9, "domain": 2, "objective": "2.1", "title": "Separate Azure durability, access, and recovery", "type": "software", "requires": ["safety"], "lore": "An Azure storage design has several independent decisions: the data service, redundancy, access control, network reachability, and recovery policy. Locally redundant storage maintains copies within a location, while zone-redundant storage spreads copies across availability zones in the primary region. Availability and service support vary, so verify the selected region and account capabilities before deployment. Replication is not the same as a backup: an authorized deletion or bad overwrite may be replicated too.", "flow": ["Define failure", "Choose redundancy", "Configure access", "Test recovery"], "example": "An application reaches a private endpoint but cannot read a blob. Network reachability does not establish data authorization. Inspect the application identity and blob permissions before broadening network exposure.", "sources": [{"title": "Azure Administrator official certification guide", "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/credentials/certifications/resources/study-guides/az-104"}], "lab": ["Create a paper storage design for zone failure and accidental overwrite.", "Choose a redundancy approach and a separate retention/versioning policy.", "Trace a client through DNS, private endpoint, and data authorization.", "Write a restore test with original content, changed content, and an expected recovered version."], "questions": [{"prompt": "Which Azure redundancy choice spreads copies across availability zones in the primary region?", "options": ["ZRS", "A blob access tier", "A resource lock alone", "A DNS alias"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Zone-redundant storage distributes copies across supported zones.", "tier": 1, "id": "specialist9-2:q1"}, {"prompt": "Why is replication alone insufficient for an accidental overwrite recovery plan?", "options": ["Replication disables all access control", "Replication is only a naming service", "Replicas never receive writes", "An unwanted change may also be replicated"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Versioning or a suitable recovery design addresses historic versions separately.", "tier": 1, "id": "specialist9-2:q2"}, {"prompt": "A private endpoint resolves correctly and is reachable, but a blob read returns an authorization error. What should be checked next?", "options": ["The color of the resource icon", "The VM display resolution", "The client identity and data permissions", "Whether the endpoint is public"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "A working path does not supply permission to read data.", "tier": 2, "id": "specialist9-2:q3"}, {"prompt": "When should a storage deletion-recovery feature be configured?", "options": ["Only during billing export", "Before the incident, with a suitable retention period", "Only after every copy is deleted", "After disabling all logs"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Recovery controls generally cannot retroactively recover data outside their protection.", "tier": 2, "id": "specialist9-2:q4"}]}, {"id": "specialist9-3", "core": 9, "domain": 3, "objective": "3.1", "title": "Trace an Azure application failure", "type": "software", "requires": ["safety"], "lore": "Troubleshooting a cloud application starts with the failing conversation, not a random restart. Identify source, destination, protocol, port, and name resolution. Azure network security groups evaluate rules using priority; lower numeric values have higher priority. Check effective rules at relevant interfaces and subnets as well as routes and service configuration. An open rule does not create a listener, and a healthy VM power state does not prove an application is serving requests.", "flow": ["Resolve name", "Trace routes and rules", "Check listener", "Correlate metrics and logs", "Verify repair"], "example": "A VM is running, but HTTPS fails after a network-rule update. Power state does not establish end-to-end service availability. Compare effective rules, the route, and the listener; verify an actual HTTPS request after repair.", "sources": [{"title": "Azure Administrator official certification guide", "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/credentials/certifications/resources/study-guides/az-104"}], "lab": ["Draw a client, subnet, VM, and HTTPS service with port 443.", "Write conflicting NSG rules with priorities 100 and 200 and predict the matching decision.", "Build a failure timeline with one management change and one application symptom.", "Define an alert condition, recipient, and a test proving the alert works."], "questions": [{"prompt": "Two matching Azure NSG rules have priorities 100 and 200. Which is considered first?", "options": ["100", "200", "The rule with the longest name", "Both are always ignored"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Lower priority numbers are evaluated first.", "tier": 1, "id": "specialist9-3:q1"}, {"prompt": "Where would you first look for an Azure resource configuration change event?", "options": ["Only a browser bookmark", "The VM wallpaper", "A blob access tier", "Activity Log"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Activity Log contains management-plane operations.", "tier": 1, "id": "specialist9-3:q2"}, {"prompt": "What does a running VM state establish about HTTPS availability?", "options": ["Every client can reach port 443", "The app has passed a transaction test", "Only part of the dependency chain is healthy", "The certificate must be valid"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Power state does not verify rules, routing, listener, certificate, or application behavior.", "tier": 2, "id": "specialist9-3:q3"}, {"prompt": "A metric alert fires but nobody receives a notification. What is a focused next check?", "options": ["Whether every port is open", "The action group and notification delivery", "The VM hostname length", "The storage redundancy setting"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "An alert condition and its response actions are distinct parts of the design.", "tier": 2, "id": "specialist9-3:q4"}]}, {"id": "specialist10-1", "core": 10, "domain": 1, "objective": "1.1", "title": "Design AWS temporary access and trust", "type": "software", "requires": ["safety"], "lore": "AWS IAM policies describe allowed or denied actions on resources, sometimes with conditions. The effective decision depends on applicable policies and evaluation rules; an explicit deny overrides an allow. A role has a trust policy describing who may assume it and permission policies describing what the resulting session may do. These are different questions. A workload should normally use a suitable role and temporary credentials rather than a long-lived access key embedded in application code.", "flow": ["Identify workload", "Establish trust", "Grant narrow actions", "Obtain temporary session", "Test effective access"], "example": "A role can read ordinary objects but cannot read a KMS-encrypted object. Object permission alone may not authorize use of the encryption key. Inspect KMS permissions and key policy alongside the S3 permission.", "sources": [{"title": "AWS Solutions Architect Associate official certification guide", "url": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/aws-certification/latest/solutions-architect-associate-03.html"}], "lab": ["Draw a fictional workload role and a single permitted S3 bucket.", "Write a task-to-permission table and identify who can assume the role.", "Add an encrypted object and document the additional key-access dependency.", "Predict an allowed read, denied write, and explicitly denied read."], "questions": [{"prompt": "What does an IAM role trust policy principally define?", "options": ["Who may assume the role", "The monthly service price", "Every network route", "The object retention period"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Trust controls role assumption; permission policies govern actions by the assumed session.", "tier": 1, "id": "specialist10-1:q1"}, {"prompt": "Which credential approach best fits an EC2 application accessing AWS services?", "options": ["A root access key in the source repository", "A shared permanent admin key in a script", "A password stored in a public bucket", "An appropriate instance role with temporary credentials"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Workload roles avoid distributing long-lived access keys.", "tier": 1, "id": "specialist10-1:q2"}, {"prompt": "An applicable explicit deny conflicts with an allow. What is the authorization result?", "options": ["Chosen randomly", "Determined by policy name length", "Denied", "Always allowed"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Explicit deny overrides an applicable allow.", "tier": 2, "id": "specialist10-1:q3"}, {"prompt": "S3 object permission is present but a customer-managed KMS encrypted read fails. Which dependency matters?", "options": ["A larger subnet", "Permission to use the KMS key", "The EC2 instance name tag alone", "The browser cache"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Encrypted access can involve both storage and key authorization.", "tier": 2, "id": "specialist10-1:q4"}]}, {"id": "specialist10-2", "core": 10, "domain": 2, "objective": "2.1", "title": "Design for failures across availability zones", "type": "software", "requires": ["safety"], "lore": "A resilient application must tolerate failures in each dependency, not just duplicate its web servers. An Application Load Balancer can distribute requests to healthy targets across configured availability zones. An Auto Scaling group can replace unhealthy capacity, but the application should avoid keeping essential session state only on a replaceable instance. Externalize state in a suitable shared service and make request handling resilient to retries.", "flow": ["Define failure target", "Distribute capacity", "Externalize state", "Decouple work", "Test failover"], "example": "Web instances span two zones, but carts exist only in local process memory. Replacing a failed instance loses its users’ carts. Move required session state to an appropriate shared service and test recovery.", "sources": [{"title": "AWS Solutions Architect Associate official certification guide", "url": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/aws-certification/latest/solutions-architect-associate-03.html"}], "lab": ["Draw two availability zones, a load balancer, replaceable app instances, and a database.", "Identify all session state and remove single-instance dependencies on paper.", "Add a queue and define how duplicate message processing will be detected.", "Walk through losing one zone and record expected user-visible behavior."], "questions": [{"prompt": "What should be avoided on replaceable app instances when sessions must survive failures?", "options": ["Keeping essential session state only in local memory", "Using a load balancer", "Health checks", "Separating compute and storage"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Local session state disappears with the instance unless deliberately preserved elsewhere.", "tier": 1, "id": "specialist10-2:q1"}, {"prompt": "What is the standby in a conventional RDS Multi-AZ DB instance deployment primarily for?", "options": ["Serving all read-only queries as a read replica", "Hosting DNS", "Replacing IAM", "Failover availability"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "The standby provides high availability; read replicas are a distinct scaling mechanism.", "tier": 1, "id": "specialist10-2:q2"}, {"prompt": "Which component can buffer work when processing is temporarily unavailable?", "options": ["A route table description", "A password policy", "A queue", "An instance name tag"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "A queue decouples arrival from processing capacity.", "tier": 2, "id": "specialist10-2:q3"}, {"prompt": "Why should a queue consumer often be idempotent?", "options": ["It replaces encryption", "A message may be delivered again", "Queues always delete databases", "Every message arrives only once under all failures"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Safe repeated processing prevents duplicate business side effects.", "tier": 2, "id": "specialist10-2:q4"}]}, {"id": "specialist10-3", "core": 10, "domain": 3, "objective": "3.1", "title": "Match AWS storage to access and recovery needs", "type": "software", "requires": ["safety"], "lore": "Storage selection starts with how an application accesses data. S3 provides object storage, EBS provides block volumes for supported compute use cases, and EFS provides a shared file system. An application expecting file-system operations cannot always be moved to an object API without changes. Durability, access latency, sharing, capacity, and recovery requirements all matter. A low storage price alone does not establish the lowest total cost.", "flow": ["Identify interface", "Estimate access pattern", "Choose storage", "Model total cost", "Test restore"], "example": "An archive must support immediate incident retrieval, but the proposed class requires a restore delay. The storage price is lower but the access requirement is not met. Select an appropriate access class or revise the recovery requirement explicitly.", "sources": [{"title": "AWS Solutions Architect Associate official certification guide", "url": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/aws-certification/latest/solutions-architect-associate-03.html"}], "lab": ["Classify an image archive, a VM disk, and a shared Linux directory by access interface.", "Write retrieval frequency and maximum acceptable recovery time for each.", "Compare immediate access with delayed archive restoration for the image archive.", "Estimate storage, requests, retrieval, and transfer categories without creating resources."], "questions": [{"prompt": "Which AWS service supplies an object storage interface?", "options": ["S3", "EBS", "EFS", "An IAM role"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "S3 stores and retrieves objects rather than exposing a block disk or shared file system.", "tier": 1, "id": "specialist10-3:q1"}, {"prompt": "An application requires a shared file system for Linux clients. Which service is the closest fit?", "options": ["An S3 bucket without application changes", "An IAM group", "A route table", "EFS"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "EFS provides a managed shared file system.", "tier": 1, "id": "specialist10-3:q2"}, {"prompt": "Which cost category can matter when archived data is unexpectedly read in bulk?", "options": ["Only the IAM policy length", "Only the dashboard color", "Retrieval and transfer charges", "Only the account display name"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Total cost includes access and transfer, not just storage capacity.", "tier": 2, "id": "specialist10-3:q3"}, {"prompt": "A recovery plan requires immediate access. What must be checked before choosing an archive class?", "options": ["Only the region abbreviation", "Whether retrieval requires a restore and its latency", "Only the class name", "Only the number of object tags"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Archive access behavior must satisfy the recovery-time requirement.", "tier": 2, "id": "specialist10-3:q4"}]}, {"id": "specialist11-1", "core": 11, "domain": 1, "objective": "1.1", "title": "Distinguish identity, verification, and authorization", "type": "security", "requires": ["safety"], "lore": "An identity identifies a person, workload, or device. Authentication establishes confidence in that identity; authorization determines what it may access. Microsoft Entra ID is a cloud identity service. Multifactor authentication combines suitable factors and reduces risks from a stolen password, but it does not replace appropriate access permissions or protect every possible session compromise. Single sign-on reduces repeated sign-ins across supported applications without removing the need for access decisions.", "flow": ["Identify actor", "Authenticate", "Evaluate access policy", "Authorize resource", "Monitor session"], "example": "A contractor signs in successfully but cannot open a finance application. Authentication succeeded; application authorization may still be absent. Verify the intended assignment rather than treating all successful sign-ins as universal access.", "sources": [{"title": "Security, Compliance, and Identity Fundamentals official certification guide", "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/credentials/certifications/resources/study-guides/sc-900"}], "lab": ["List fictional staff, contractor, and workload identities.", "Build an application access matrix with the minimum required privileges.", "Write normal and risky sign-in scenarios and choose verification requirements.", "Document how policy testing avoids locking out emergency access."], "questions": [{"prompt": "A successful sign-in establishes which part of access?", "options": ["Authentication", "Permission to every file", "Universal administrator rights", "Data classification"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Authentication identifies the actor; authorization is a separate decision.", "tier": 1, "id": "specialist11-1:q1"}, {"prompt": "Which Microsoft service is principally associated with cloud identity?", "options": ["A storage access tier", "A virtual network route", "An archive mailbox alone", "Microsoft Entra ID"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Entra ID manages cloud identities and access capabilities.", "tier": 1, "id": "specialist11-1:q2"}, {"prompt": "What is a goal of least privilege?", "options": ["Avoid all verification", "Trust every corporate IP indefinitely", "Grant only the access needed for the task", "Give every employee administrator access"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Narrow permissions reduce unnecessary access and impact.", "tier": 2, "id": "specialist11-1:q3"}, {"prompt": "What does single sign-on normally reduce?", "options": ["All need for monitoring", "Repeated sign-in prompts across supported applications", "Every authorization decision", "All endpoint risk"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "SSO simplifies authentication experience without granting universal access.", "tier": 2, "id": "specialist11-1:q4"}]}, {"id": "specialist11-2", "core": 11, "domain": 2, "objective": "2.1", "title": "Choose threat protection by the evidence needed", "type": "security", "requires": ["safety"], "lore": "Security products address different evidence and response needs. Microsoft Defender capabilities cover areas such as endpoints, email, identities, and cloud workloads. Extended detection and response correlates signals across supported security domains. Microsoft Sentinel is a cloud-native SIEM and SOAR platform that can collect and analyze data from multiple sources and support response workflows. The name of a product alone does not mean that every source is connected or every response is enabled.", "flow": ["Collect signals", "Correlate entities", "Investigate incident", "Authorize response", "Verify result"], "example": "A suspicious email and an endpoint alert reference the same user within minutes. Correlation strengthens an investigation but still needs entity and time validation. Inspect linked evidence before declaring the scope or automating disruptive containment.", "sources": [{"title": "Security, Compliance, and Identity Fundamentals official certification guide", "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/credentials/certifications/resources/study-guides/sc-900"}], "lab": ["Create fictional email, endpoint, and identity alerts for one user.", "Normalize times and identify which signals refer to the same device or session.", "Assign evidence sources to an investigation and write one benign alternative.", "Define a response action with an owner, authority, and verification step."], "questions": [{"prompt": "Which Microsoft platform is associated with SIEM and SOAR?", "options": ["Microsoft Sentinel", "Azure DNS alone", "A file compression utility", "A resource tag"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Sentinel supports security analytics and orchestration across connected data.", "tier": 1, "id": "specialist11-2:q1"}, {"prompt": "What does XDR aim to correlate?", "options": ["Only billing invoices", "Only screen resolutions", "Only static passwords", "Signals across supported security domains"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Cross-domain correlation can link endpoint, identity, email, and other security evidence.", "tier": 1, "id": "specialist11-2:q2"}, {"prompt": "What does a single security alert establish?", "options": ["The full business impact", "That response authorization is unnecessary", "A condition requiring investigation", "Guaranteed compromise of all systems"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "An alert needs context and corroboration.", "tier": 2, "id": "specialist11-2:q3"}, {"prompt": "Before an automated account block, what should the response design include?", "options": ["Removal of audit records", "Authority, scope, and verification", "Only a memorable rule name", "A public password list"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Automation can be disruptive and needs deliberate boundaries.", "tier": 2, "id": "specialist11-2:q4"}]}, {"id": "specialist11-3", "core": 11, "domain": 3, "objective": "3.1", "title": "Separate classification, retention, and leakage prevention", "type": "security", "requires": ["safety"], "lore": "Data protection begins by understanding what data exists and how it should be handled. Sensitivity labels can classify content and, when configured, apply protection such as encryption or access restrictions. The label name alone does not prove a particular protection setting. Data loss prevention policies can identify sensitive content and apply actions to risky handling across supported locations. A policy must be scoped and tested so legitimate work is not unnecessarily disrupted.", "flow": ["Discover data", "Classify sensitivity", "Set sharing controls", "Set retention", "Test outcomes"], "example": "A document is labeled Confidential, but external access still works. The label may not be configured with the assumed enforcement. Inspect actual label protection and sharing policies instead of relying on its name.", "sources": [{"title": "Security, Compliance, and Identity Fundamentals official certification guide", "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/credentials/certifications/resources/study-guides/sc-900"}], "lab": ["Create fictional public, internal, and payroll document descriptions.", "Assign a sensitivity category and permitted recipients for each.", "Write a DLP action for an external payroll share and a separate retention rule.", "Test an authorized exception and a false-positive case on paper."], "questions": [{"prompt": "Which control is most directly concerned with identifying risky transfers of sensitive data?", "options": ["Data loss prevention", "A VM size", "A DNS TTL", "A load-balancer probe"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "DLP policies address sensitive-data handling across supported locations.", "tier": 1, "id": "specialist11-3:q1"}, {"prompt": "What does a retention policy principally address?", "options": ["Which VM has most CPU", "How DNS resolves names", "Whether every password is strong", "How long content is kept or deleted"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Retention governs the content lifecycle.", "tier": 1, "id": "specialist11-3:q2"}, {"prompt": "A sensitivity label is named Confidential. What can be concluded from the name alone?", "options": ["Every copy is automatically deleted", "The organization is compliant with every law", "The intended category, not guaranteed encryption settings", "All external sharing is technically impossible"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Actual protection depends on configuration and supported behavior.", "tier": 2, "id": "specialist11-3:q3"}, {"prompt": "Why test a DLP policy before broad enforcement?", "options": ["To make every document public", "To validate scope and unintended blocking", "To eliminate the need for owners", "To remove all evidence"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Policy testing exposes false positives and workflow impact.", "tier": 2, "id": "specialist11-3:q4"}]}, {"id": "specialist12-1", "core": 12, "domain": 1, "objective": "1.1", "title": "Connect cloud choices to business outcomes", "type": "software", "requires": ["safety"], "lore": "Cloud transformation is a business change supported by technology, not simply moving the same machines elsewhere. Define the outcome first: shorter release cycles, improved resilience, flexible capacity, or better access to data. Infrastructure as a service leaves more operating-system and application responsibilities with the customer. Managed platforms can reduce some operational work, while software as a service offers an application with a different control and responsibility boundary. Every model still requires appropriate customer decisions about data and access.", "flow": ["Define outcome", "Identify constraints", "Compare operating models", "Estimate total cost", "Measure results"], "example": "A company moves idle servers to cloud VMs and its bill increases. Migration alone did not right-size resources or change operating practices. Measure utilization and redesign capacity against the business need.", "sources": [{"title": "Google Cloud Digital Leader official certification guide", "url": "https://cloud.google.com/learn/certification/cloud-digital-leader"}], "lab": ["Choose a fictional shop with seasonal traffic and a slow release process.", "Define two measurable business goals and a dependency constraint.", "Compare keeping servers, using managed hosting, and using a hosted application.", "List cost categories and define how the chosen outcome will be measured."], "questions": [{"prompt": "What should guide a cloud transformation choice first?", "options": ["A measurable business outcome and constraints", "The newest product name", "The longest service list", "The number of dashboard widgets"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Technology choices should support explicit business needs.", "tier": 1, "id": "specialist12-1:q1"}, {"prompt": "What does elasticity describe?", "options": ["Guaranteed zero cost", "Removing every security duty", "Keeping a fixed server count forever", "Adjusting capacity with demand"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Elastic capacity can respond to workload changes.", "tier": 1, "id": "specialist12-1:q2"}, {"prompt": "Which cost belongs in a migration comparison beyond compute usage?", "options": ["Only the project abbreviation", "Only the console font", "Migration and operational effort", "Only the logo design"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Total cost includes transition and ongoing operating work.", "tier": 2, "id": "specialist12-1:q3"}, {"prompt": "Does using a managed cloud service remove the customer responsibility for access decisions?", "options": ["Only during the first month", "No, customer access and data decisions still matter", "Yes, all customer duties disappear", "Only if the name contains serverless"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Service models change the responsibility boundary.", "tier": 2, "id": "specialist12-1:q4"}]}, {"id": "specialist12-2", "core": 12, "domain": 2, "objective": "2.1", "title": "Choose data and AI approaches responsibly", "type": "software", "requires": ["safety"], "lore": "Data projects begin with a question and trustworthy information. An analytical warehouse supports large-scale analysis, while a transactional application database is designed around operational reads and writes. BigQuery is Google Cloud's managed analytics warehouse; selecting it does not by itself fix missing, biased, duplicated, or poorly governed data. Identify source quality, ownership, access rights, and update needs before drawing conclusions from a dashboard.", "flow": ["Define question", "Check data quality", "Select approach", "Evaluate on representative cases", "Monitor and review"], "example": "A generated support response sounds confident but invents a product feature. Fluency is not a correctness check. Validate against an approved knowledge source and require review for unsupported claims.", "sources": [{"title": "Google Cloud Digital Leader official certification guide", "url": "https://cloud.google.com/learn/certification/cloud-digital-leader"}], "lab": ["Describe a sales-reporting task, a demand-prediction task, and a drafting task.", "Choose an approach for each and name the required data.", "Create five fictional evaluation cases including a failure or underrepresented case.", "Define accuracy evidence, privacy boundaries, and a human-review trigger."], "questions": [{"prompt": "Which Google Cloud service is associated with managed analytical warehousing?", "options": ["BigQuery", "A DNS record", "An IAM role", "A virtual firewall rule"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "BigQuery supports analytical queries and data warehousing.", "tier": 1, "id": "specialist12-2:q1"}, {"prompt": "What should be checked before interpreting a sales dashboard?", "options": ["Only chart colors", "Only the dashboard title", "Only the browser zoom", "Data quality, completeness, and definitions"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Poor input quality or inconsistent definitions can mislead analysis.", "tier": 1, "id": "specialist12-2:q2"}, {"prompt": "What does fluent generative AI output prove?", "options": ["No human review is ever needed", "The source data is unbiased", "Neither factual accuracy nor suitability by itself", "Every fact is verified"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Generated content needs appropriate evaluation and grounding.", "tier": 2, "id": "specialist12-2:q3"}, {"prompt": "Why use representative evaluation cases separate from training examples?", "options": ["To remove privacy requirements", "To better assess generalization", "To guarantee zero errors", "To avoid defining success"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Testing only memorized examples can overstate real-world performance.", "tier": 2, "id": "specialist12-2:q4"}]}, {"id": "specialist12-3", "core": 12, "domain": 3, "objective": "3.1", "title": "Plan Google Cloud responsibility and reliability", "type": "software", "requires": ["safety"], "lore": "Cloud providers secure parts of the underlying platform, but customers retain responsibilities that depend on the service. Identity permissions, data handling, and application configuration remain central. IAM roles should match actual duties at appropriate scopes, and identities used by workloads need deliberate permissions too. Do not equate provider infrastructure security with a correctly secured application. Logging and monitoring must provide evidence relevant to the service's operation.", "flow": ["Assign responsibilities", "Limit access", "Choose reliability measure", "Set target", "Test recovery and review cost"], "example": "A team receives a budget notification and assumes all spending has stopped. A notification is not a universal hard cap. Inspect usage and apply the intended cost-control actions explicitly.", "sources": [{"title": "Google Cloud Digital Leader official certification guide", "url": "https://cloud.google.com/learn/certification/cloud-digital-leader"}], "lab": ["Draw provider and customer responsibility columns for a managed application.", "Define a successful-request indicator and a target.", "Write a backup restore test and distinguish it from automatic failover.", "Assign a cost owner and an action to take when a budget notification arrives."], "questions": [{"prompt": "Which is a customer responsibility even when using managed cloud services?", "options": ["Appropriate access and data configuration", "Maintaining every provider building", "Replacing every physical disk", "Operating the provider power grid"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Responsibility depends on service, but customer configuration and data decisions remain.", "tier": 1, "id": "specialist12-3:q1"}, {"prompt": "What is a service-level indicator?", "options": ["A target without measurement", "An invoice number", "A user password", "A measured service behavior"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "An SLI is a quantitative measure, such as successful requests.", "tier": 1, "id": "specialist12-3:q2"}, {"prompt": "What is a service-level objective?", "options": ["A guaranteed free service", "A log entry filename", "A target for a service-level indicator", "A physical network cable"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "An SLO establishes the desired level of measured performance.", "tier": 2, "id": "specialist12-3:q3"}, {"prompt": "Why test restoring a backup?", "options": ["To turn every backup into automatic failover", "To verify usable recovery and timing", "To replace all access control", "To guarantee no future failures"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Restore testing provides evidence of recovery capability.", "tier": 2, "id": "specialist12-3:q4"}]}, {"id": "specialist13-1", "core": 13, "domain": 1, "objective": "1.1", "title": "Read the Terraform change workflow", "type": "software", "requires": ["safety"], "lore": "Terraform describes desired infrastructure in configuration and uses providers to interact with platform APIs. A provider implements resource and data-source operations. A resource represents something Terraform manages; a data source reads information for use in configuration. Initialization prepares the working directory and required providers or modules. Validation checks configuration consistency, but it does not establish that a proposed infrastructure change is safe or permitted by a remote API.", "flow": ["Write configuration", "Initialize", "Validate", "Review plan", "Apply in lab", "Verify result"], "example": "A valid configuration proposes replacing a resource that stores required state. Syntax validity does not establish acceptable operational impact. Review the replacement and recovery requirements before applying.", "sources": [{"title": "Terraform Associate official certification guide", "url": "https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/tutorials/certification-004/associate-review-004"}], "lab": ["Create a disposable directory or use a paper local_file example.", "Describe a local file resource with a filename and harmless content; identify its provider.", "Predict init, validate, plan, and apply outcomes before running anything.", "Change only the content and compare the planned action with the resulting file."], "questions": [{"prompt": "Which Terraform command initializes required providers for a working directory?", "options": ["terraform init", "terraform output", "terraform fmt alone", "terraform state list"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Initialization prepares provider and module dependencies.", "tier": 1, "id": "specialist13-1:q1"}, {"prompt": "What does a Terraform data source principally do?", "options": ["Always create a new managed resource", "Encrypt every state file", "Replace every provider", "Read information for configuration"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Data sources query information rather than necessarily managing a new object.", "tier": 1, "id": "specialist13-1:q2"}, {"prompt": "What should be inspected in a Terraform plan before applying?", "options": ["Only terminal colors", "Only the directory icon", "Creates, updates, replacements, and destroys", "Only the file name length"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "The proposed operations determine change impact.", "tier": 2, "id": "specialist13-1:q3"}, {"prompt": "Does terraform validate prove a deployment is operationally safe?", "options": ["Only when the filename is main.tf", "No, plan and impact review are still needed", "Yes, it proves every remote permission", "Yes, it guarantees no downtime"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Configuration validity does not prove runtime effects or suitability.", "tier": 2, "id": "specialist13-1:q4"}]}, {"id": "specialist13-2", "core": 13, "domain": 2, "objective": "2.1", "title": "Protect Terraform state and coordinate changes", "type": "software", "requires": ["safety"], "lore": "Terraform state maps configuration addresses to managed objects and records information needed for subsequent operations. It can contain sensitive values. Marking an output sensitive suppresses ordinary display but is not a guarantee that the value is absent from state. Protect state access and storage, and avoid committing state or secrets to a source repository. Configuration belongs in version control; state needs its own controlled handling.", "flow": ["Identify state", "Restrict access", "Coordinate writers", "Review plan", "Verify and preserve recovery"], "example": "A sensitive output is hidden in console output, so a developer proposes committing the state file. Suppressed display does not mean the state excludes the value. Keep state in a controlled backend and review secret exposure separately.", "sources": [{"title": "Terraform Associate official certification guide", "url": "https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/tutorials/certification-004/associate-review-004"}], "lab": ["Draw two operators sharing one infrastructure workspace.", "List data that state may contain and who should access it.", "Write a sequence where one apply holds a lock while another waits.", "Describe how to investigate an interrupted operation without unlocking an active writer."], "questions": [{"prompt": "What does Terraform state help maintain?", "options": ["The mapping between configuration addresses and managed objects", "Only terminal colors", "The legal owner of every cloud account", "Every user browser history"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "State tracks managed-object associations and information needed for operations.", "tier": 1, "id": "specialist13-2:q1"}, {"prompt": "Does marking a Terraform output sensitive ensure the secret is absent from state?", "options": ["Yes, always", "Only if it has a short name", "Only when printed twice", "No"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Sensitive marking suppresses display; state may still contain the value.", "tier": 1, "id": "specialist13-2:q2"}, {"prompt": "What can state locking help prevent?", "options": ["Every provider outage", "All billing charges", "Concurrent conflicting state operations", "Every insecure IAM policy"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Locking coordinates writers where the backend supports it.", "tier": 2, "id": "specialist13-2:q3"}, {"prompt": "Before force-unlocking after an interruption, what must be established?", "options": ["Only the number of comments", "Whether an operation is still active", "Only the directory name", "Whether the terminal is maximized"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Removing an active lock can allow unsafe concurrent operations.", "tier": 2, "id": "specialist13-2:q4"}]}, {"id": "specialist13-3", "core": 13, "domain": 3, "objective": "3.1", "title": "Build reusable Terraform inputs and outputs", "type": "software", "requires": ["safety"], "lore": "A Terraform module groups configuration into a reusable unit. Input variables define values supplied by a caller, while outputs expose selected results. Clear variable types and validation rules help catch unintended values before changes are applied. A module should have an understandable contract rather than requiring callers to inspect every internal resource. Provider requirements and version constraints help communicate compatibility.", "flow": ["Define module contract", "Validate inputs", "Select versions", "Reference outputs", "Review instance addresses"], "example": "A team removes the first item from a count-indexed list and sees unexpected instance address changes. Index-based identity follows positions rather than business names. Review whether stable for_each keys better express the intended identities.", "sources": [{"title": "Terraform Associate official certification guide", "url": "https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/tutorials/certification-004/associate-review-004"}], "lab": ["Sketch a local-file module with a filename input and a path output.", "Add a type and a validation rule for an allowed label.", "Compare numeric count addresses with for_each keys for a changing set.", "Write an upgrade checklist covering provider selection and module constraints."], "questions": [{"prompt": "What is an input variable used for in a Terraform module?", "options": ["Accept a value from the caller", "Automatically expose every secret", "Replace all state", "Disable provider requirements"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Inputs define configurable values for a module.", "tier": 1, "id": "specialist13-3:q1"}, {"prompt": "What does an output provide to a module caller?", "options": ["An automatic administrator credential", "A replacement for every input", "A command history archive", "A selected result from the module"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Outputs expose selected values for other configuration or operators.", "tier": 1, "id": "specialist13-3:q2"}, {"prompt": "What does the Terraform dependency lock file primarily record?", "options": ["All managed resource state", "Every cloud password", "Provider selections and checksums", "Every remote module version universally"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "The dependency lock file concerns provider dependency selections.", "tier": 2, "id": "specialist13-3:q3"}, {"prompt": "What is a benefit of stable for_each keys?", "options": ["They make all dependencies manual", "Instance identity can follow meaningful keys", "They eliminate every replacement", "They disable validation"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Stable keys avoid relying solely on list positions for instance identity.", "tier": 2, "id": "specialist13-3:q4"}]}, {"id": "specialist14-1", "core": 14, "domain": 1, "objective": "1.1", "title": "Use Linux ownership and permissions deliberately", "type": "physical", "requires": ["safety"], "lore": "Linux file access depends on identity, ownership, permission bits, and potentially additional controls such as ACLs and SELinux. Basic mode bits distinguish owner, group, and others. Read, write, and execute have context-specific meaning: execute on a directory allows traversal, while read allows listing its entries. A process may need traversal permission on every parent directory before it can open a file. Avoid changing everything to mode 777 to hide an access problem.", "flow": ["Identify user", "Inspect path ownership", "Check parent traversal", "Change narrow permissions", "Test as intended user"], "example": "A test user has file read permission but cannot open it through a parent directory. Parent-directory traversal may be denied even when the file itself is readable. Inspect each path component and grant only the intended traversal access.", "sources": [{"title": "RHCSA · Red Hat System Administrator official certification guide", "url": "https://www.redhat.com/en/services/training/ex200-red-hat-certified-system-administrator-rhcsa-exam"}], "lab": ["In a disposable Linux VM, create a dedicated lab directory and harmless text file.", "Record id and ls -l output and explain owner, group, and other permissions.", "Compare directory modes with and without execute using a non-root test identity.", "Restore the lab permissions and document why chmod 777 was unnecessary."], "questions": [{"prompt": "Which Linux command changes file mode bits?", "options": ["chmod", "chown", "pwd", "hostname"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "chmod changes permission bits; chown changes ownership.", "tier": 1, "id": "specialist14-1:q1"}, {"prompt": "What does execute permission on a Linux directory permit?", "options": ["Editing every file regardless of its permissions", "Reading all file contents automatically", "Changing every owner", "Traversal through the directory"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Directory execute is required to traverse the path.", "tier": 1, "id": "specialist14-1:q2"}, {"prompt": "Which command reports the current identity and group memberships?", "options": ["free", "uname -r", "id", "df"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "id helps determine which ownership class applies.", "tier": 2, "id": "specialist14-1:q3"}, {"prompt": "Does changing umask retroactively change every existing file mode?", "options": ["Only for text files", "No", "Yes, immediately", "Only on Mondays"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "umask affects defaults for new object creation, not existing modes.", "tier": 2, "id": "specialist14-1:q4"}]}, {"id": "specialist14-2", "core": 14, "domain": 2, "objective": "2.1", "title": "Verify Linux services now and after reboot", "type": "physical", "requires": ["safety"], "lore": "A systemd service has both current runtime state and enablement behavior. Starting a service affects the running system; enabling it arranges startup according to its unit installation configuration. Enabling alone does not necessarily start it immediately, and starting alone does not guarantee startup after reboot. Inspect the unit with systemctl status and relevant properties, and review logs with journalctl for the service. Use a harmless existing service in a disposable VM rather than changing a production host.", "flow": ["Inspect service", "Read unit logs", "Identify cause", "Apply narrow correction", "Verify now and after reboot"], "example": "A service works after systemctl start but is absent after a reboot. The runtime change may not have established startup enablement. Inspect enablement and unit configuration, then verify persistence with a controlled reboot.", "sources": [{"title": "RHCSA · Red Hat System Administrator official certification guide", "url": "https://www.redhat.com/en/services/training/ex200-red-hat-certified-system-administrator-rhcsa-exam"}], "lab": ["Choose a harmless service in a disposable VM and record its current state and enablement.", "Explain the effects of start, stop, enable, and disable before any change.", "Inspect service-specific logs and write a targeted diagnosis of a fictional failed start.", "Verify the intended startup behavior after a controlled VM reboot, then restore the starting settings."], "questions": [{"prompt": "Which command family manages systemd services?", "options": ["systemctl", "chmod", "tar", "du"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "systemctl inspects and controls systemd units.", "tier": 1, "id": "specialist14-2:q1"}, {"prompt": "What does starting a service establish by itself?", "options": ["Permanent startup under every target", "All firewall access", "A successful remote transaction", "Its current runtime attempt, not guaranteed reboot persistence"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Start and enablement are distinct operations.", "tier": 1, "id": "specialist14-2:q2"}, {"prompt": "Which tool is commonly used to inspect systemd journal entries for a service?", "options": ["passwd", "lsblk alone", "journalctl", "mkfs"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "journalctl can filter entries by unit and other criteria.", "tier": 2, "id": "specialist14-2:q3"}, {"prompt": "A firewall permits a port but nothing listens there. What follows?", "options": ["DNS will start the process", "The application still needs to start and bind a listener", "The firewall creates the service automatically", "Every client request must succeed"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Network permission does not create an application listener.", "tier": 2, "id": "specialist14-2:q4"}]}, {"id": "specialist14-3", "core": 14, "domain": 3, "objective": "3.1", "title": "Plan persistent Linux mounts and storage verification", "type": "physical", "requires": ["safety"], "lore": "Linux storage involves several layers: a block device or logical volume, a file system, a mount point, and mount configuration. Formatting creates a file system and can destroy existing contents, so the introductory exercise should use a disposable VM disk or a paper plan, never an unidentified host disk. lsblk and blkid help inspect devices, file systems, and identifiers. A directory existing does not prove that the intended file system is mounted there; inspect mount information and capacity.", "flow": ["Identify disposable device", "Inspect file system", "Plan mount", "Validate persistent configuration", "Reboot and verify"], "example": "A mount directory exists after reboot, but data is written to the root file system instead. Directory existence did not prove the intended volume was mounted. Inspect the actual mount source and correct the persistent mount configuration.", "sources": [{"title": "RHCSA · Red Hat System Administrator official certification guide", "url": "https://www.redhat.com/en/services/training/ex200-red-hat-certified-system-administrator-rhcsa-exam"}], "lab": ["Use a paper device table or a disposable VM disk clearly marked for this exercise.", "Record the intended device, file-system type, UUID, and mount point.", "Draft an fstab entry and a recovery plan without formatting any existing disk.", "Define checks for actual backing storage, a harmless test file, and mount persistence after reboot."], "questions": [{"prompt": "Which Linux tool helps list block devices and their relationships?", "options": ["lsblk", "whoami", "history", "alias"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "lsblk shows block-device information and hierarchy.", "tier": 1, "id": "specialist14-3:q1"}, {"prompt": "Where are conventional persistent Linux file-system mount entries configured?", "options": ["/etc/hostname", "A shell history file", "A desktop wallpaper setting", "/etc/fstab"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "fstab provides file-system mount configuration used during startup and mounting.", "tier": 1, "id": "specialist14-3:q2"}, {"prompt": "Why use a file-system UUID in a mount configuration?", "options": ["To guarantee backups", "To remove all permission checks", "To identify the file system without relying only on changing device names", "To encrypt every file automatically"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "UUIDs provide a persistent file-system identifier.", "tier": 2, "id": "specialist14-3:q3"}, {"prompt": "Does an existing mount-point directory prove the intended disk is mounted?", "options": ["Only if root created it", "No, inspect the actual mount source", "Yes, always", "Only if the name is short"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "A plain directory can exist on the underlying file system without the desired mount.", "tier": 2, "id": "specialist14-3:q4"}]}, {"id": "specialist9-4-1", "core": 9, "domain": 4, "objective": "4.1", "title": "Deploy and inspect ARM and Bicep", "type": "software", "requires": ["safety"], "lore": "Bicep compiles to an ARM template. Both describe desired Azure resources, while the deployment command supplies a target scope and parameters. Use symbolic resource references to express dependencies. Secure parameters should not appear in ordinary outputs or source control. What-if previews intended changes, but can report unresolved properties and cannot prove application health. Inspect the actual deployment operations and validate the resulting resources.", "flow": ["A Bicep change moves a resource to another region", "Review the what-if result for replacement and assess migration before deploying", "A location change can require a new resource and a planned migration."], "example": "A Bicep change moves a resource to another region. Review the what-if result for replacement and assess migration before deploying. A location change can require a new resource and a planned migration.", "sources": [{"title": "Azure Administrator official certification guide", "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/credentials/certifications/resources/study-guides/az-104"}], "lab": ["Write a Bicep storage declaration with a location parameter", "Build it locally with az bicep build and inspect generated ARM JSON", "Mark a fictional secret input secure and ensure no output references it", "Compare an intended location change with the existing resource properties"], "questions": [{"id": "specialist9-4-1:q1", "prompt": "A Bicep change moves a resource to another region. What should you do?", "options": ["Review the what-if result for replacement and assess migration before deploying", "Run an incremental deployment because it guarantees zero downtime", "Change the resource group tag to force in-place relocation"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "A location change can require a new resource and a planned migration.", "tier": 2}, {"id": "specialist9-4-1:q2", "prompt": "A secret parameter must be passed without displaying its value in deployment output. What should you do?", "options": ["Declare it secure and keep it out of outputs", "Store it in a normal output for debugging", "Commit it to the template as a default"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Protecting inputs is separate from deciding what a template publishes.", "tier": 2}]}, {"id": "specialist9-4-2", "core": 9, "domain": 4, "objective": "4.2", "title": "Choose and operate Azure virtual machines", "type": "software", "requires": ["safety"], "lore": "VM sizing, disk type, and placement are separate choices. Availability zones isolate zone-level failures. Availability sets spread machines across fault and update domains within the supported design. A scale set manages a pool of VM instances and can scale against metrics. An attached managed disk is distinct from a temporary disk. Before resizing or moving a VM, check SKU availability, dependencies, disk limits, and disruption.", "flow": ["A service must continue during a zone failure", "Distribute healthy instances across availability zones", "Zone diversity and resilient application state are separate requirements."], "example": "A service must continue during a zone failure. Distribute healthy instances across availability zones. Zone diversity and resilient application state are separate requirements.", "sources": [{"title": "Azure Administrator official certification guide", "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/credentials/certifications/resources/study-guides/az-104"}], "lab": ["Sketch a two-zone VM deployment", "Record which disks contain OS, temporary data and durable app data", "Choose a health probe and a scaling threshold", "Write the verification steps for removing one unhealthy instance"], "questions": [{"id": "specialist9-4-2:q1", "prompt": "A service must continue during a zone failure. What should you do?", "options": ["Distribute healthy instances across availability zones", "Place both instances in a single availability set and assume zone separation", "Store all session data on one VM temporary disk"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Zone diversity and resilient application state are separate requirements.", "tier": 2}, {"id": "specialist9-4-2:q2", "prompt": "An application writes durable uploads to the VM temporary disk. What should you do?", "options": ["Move uploads to persistent storage with a recovery plan", "Add autoscaling to preserve the local files", "Resize the VM to extend file retention"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Temporary storage can be lost during lifecycle operations.", "tier": 2}]}, {"id": "specialist9-4-3", "core": 9, "domain": 4, "objective": "4.3", "title": "Run containers and stage App Service releases", "type": "software", "requires": ["safety"], "lore": "Azure Container Registry stores images. Container Instances runs containers for simple tasks, while Container Apps supports app revisions, ingress and event-driven scaling. App Service hosts web applications on a plan. Deployment slots allow staging and traffic changes where supported. Mark environment-specific settings as slot settings and verify identity, networking and certificates independently of the code deployment.", "flow": ["A team needs to test a web release before promoting it", "Use an App Service staging slot and verify slot-specific settings before swapping", "A slot provides a separate target for verification before a swap."], "example": "A team needs to test a web release before promoting it. Use an App Service staging slot and verify slot-specific settings before swapping. A slot provides a separate target for verification before a swap.", "sources": [{"title": "Azure Administrator official certification guide", "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/credentials/certifications/resources/study-guides/az-104"}], "lab": ["Create a table comparing ACR, ACI, Container Apps and App Service", "Draw a staging and production slot with a different database setting in each", "Record a readiness check and a rollback decision", "List DNS and TLS checks for the production hostname"], "questions": [{"id": "specialist9-4-3:q1", "prompt": "A team needs to test a web release before promoting it. What should you do?", "options": ["Use an App Service staging slot and verify slot-specific settings before swapping", "Replace production DNS before testing the app", "Store the release in Azure Container Registry and assume it is running"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "A slot provides a separate target for verification before a swap.", "tier": 2}, {"id": "specialist9-4-3:q2", "prompt": "An event-driven container worker needs scaling and revisions. What should you do?", "options": ["Evaluate Azure Container Apps", "Use Container Registry as the worker runtime", "Create an NSG as the scaling controller"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "A registry stores images. Container Apps runs and scales workloads.", "tier": 2}]}, {"id": "specialist9-5-1", "core": 9, "domain": 5, "objective": "5.1", "title": "Plan Azure Backup and Site Recovery", "type": "software", "requires": ["safety"], "lore": "A backup gives restore points. Replication and Site Recovery support failover to a recovery environment. Vault choice depends on workload support. Configure retention against the required recovery point, and test the recovery time with a restore. Test failover in an isolated network so the recovered systems cannot collide with production names or addresses. Validate dependencies, data integrity, and client routing before declaring recovery.", "flow": ["A regional disaster requires bringing replicated workloads online elsewhere", "Use a tested Site Recovery failover plan", "Failover requires recoverable workloads, networking and tested procedures."], "example": "A regional disaster requires bringing replicated workloads online elsewhere. Use a tested Site Recovery failover plan. Failover requires recoverable workloads, networking and tested procedures.", "sources": [{"title": "Azure Administrator official certification guide", "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/credentials/certifications/resources/study-guides/az-104"}], "lab": ["Define RPO and RTO for a fictional workload", "Choose a backup retention policy and document restore dependencies", "Draft an isolated test-failover network", "Record a checksum and application transaction as recovery evidence"], "questions": [{"id": "specialist9-5-1:q1", "prompt": "A regional disaster requires bringing replicated workloads online elsewhere. What should you do?", "options": ["Use a tested Site Recovery failover plan", "Assume a delete lock recreates a failed region", "Use only a resource group budget alert"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Failover requires recoverable workloads, networking and tested procedures.", "tier": 2}, {"id": "specialist9-5-1:q2", "prompt": "A file was corrupted yesterday and replication copied the corruption. What should you do?", "options": ["Restore a known-good historical recovery point", "Fail over only to the most recent replica", "Increase the NSG priority number"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Replication can copy corruption. A historical recovery point addresses a different failure.", "tier": 2}]}, {"id": "specialist10-4-1", "core": 10, "domain": 4, "objective": "4.1", "title": "Match compute and data services to a workload", "type": "software", "requires": ["safety"], "lore": "Select compute from runtime constraints and traffic shape. EC2 exposes host-level control, Fargate runs supported container workloads without managing hosts, and Lambda fits event-driven functions within its execution limits. DynamoDB benefits from partition keys that distribute access. RDS supports relational queries and transactions. CloudFront caches content at edge locations while ElastiCache accelerates application data access. Measure p95 latency and throttling before changing services.", "flow": ["A key-value table throttles on a single popular partition key", "Redesign key distribution and check access patterns", "A hot key can concentrate work despite spare aggregate capacity."], "example": "A key-value table throttles on a single popular partition key. Redesign key distribution and check access patterns. A hot key can concentrate work despite spare aggregate capacity.", "sources": [{"title": "AWS Solutions Architect Associate official certification guide", "url": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/aws-certification/latest/solutions-architect-associate-03.html"}], "lab": ["List read/write patterns and traffic bursts", "Choose compute, data store and cache independently", "Calculate origin requests with a stated cache hit rate", "Define a load test including p95 latency and cache misses"], "questions": [{"id": "specialist10-4-1:q1", "prompt": "A key-value table throttles on a single popular partition key. What should you do?", "options": ["Redesign key distribution and check access patterns", "Move static images into the same hot partition", "Add a CloudFront distribution and assume it changes table partitioning"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "A hot key can concentrate work despite spare aggregate capacity.", "tier": 2}, {"id": "specialist10-4-1:q2", "prompt": "Global users repeatedly download the same versioned static assets. What should you do?", "options": ["Serve cacheable objects through CloudFront", "Route every download through a primary database", "Use IAM policies to reduce physical network distance"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Edge caching reduces repeated origin work and client latency.", "tier": 2}]}, {"id": "specialist10-1-2", "core": 10, "domain": 1, "objective": "1.2", "title": "Design AWS network isolation", "type": "software", "requires": ["safety"], "lore": "A VPC subnet is public when its route table has a route to an internet gateway, but IPv4 internet access also needs an appropriate public address and allowed traffic. Private workloads may use a NAT gateway for outbound internet access or service endpoints for supported APIs. Security groups are stateful. Network ACLs are stateless and require matching return paths. Keep the database reachable from the application security group only.", "flow": ["Private workloads need S3 access without internet egress", "Use an S3 gateway endpoint and restrict access with applicable policies", "A gateway endpoint supplies a private VPC path to S3."], "example": "Private workloads need S3 access without internet egress. Use an S3 gateway endpoint and restrict access with applicable policies. A gateway endpoint supplies a private VPC path to S3.", "sources": [{"title": "AWS Solutions Architect Associate official certification guide", "url": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/aws-certification/latest/solutions-architect-associate-03.html"}], "lab": ["Draw public load balancer and private application/database subnets", "Write route tables and the two security group relationships", "Trace return traffic through both ACLs", "Add a private S3 path and identify its policy boundaries"], "questions": [{"id": "specialist10-1-2:q1", "prompt": "Private workloads need S3 access without internet egress. What should you do?", "options": ["Use an S3 gateway endpoint and restrict access with applicable policies", "Assign public IP addresses to the database tier", "Add a public DNS record for the bucket and remove all route rules"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "A gateway endpoint supplies a private VPC path to S3.", "tier": 2}, {"id": "specialist10-1-2:q2", "prompt": "A stateless ACL allows a request but drops the response. What should you do?", "options": ["Inspect return-direction ephemeral-port rules", "Assume all ACLs track established connections", "Add an IAM role to reopen the network path"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Stateless network ACL evaluation applies independently to each direction.", "tier": 2}]}, {"id": "specialist10-2-2", "core": 10, "domain": 2, "objective": "2.2", "title": "Compare AWS disaster recovery designs", "type": "software", "requires": ["safety"], "lore": "Backup and restore, pilot light, warm standby and active-active represent different recovery commitments. A pilot light keeps critical components ready while a warm standby keeps a scaled-down working environment. Multi-AZ protects against some local failures, while multi-region design can address regional outages. Establish RPO and RTO by business impact, and test client routing, data consistency and capacity during recovery.", "flow": ["The recovery region must already serve a small live workload", "Use a warm standby design", "Warm standby keeps a functional scaled-down environment."], "example": "The recovery region must already serve a small live workload. Use a warm standby design. Warm standby keeps a functional scaled-down environment.", "sources": [{"title": "AWS Solutions Architect Associate official certification guide", "url": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/aws-certification/latest/solutions-architect-associate-03.html"}], "lab": ["Rank four DR strategies for a fictional billing system", "Estimate data loss and restoration time", "Document how to route traffic and prevent conflicting writes", "Perform a tabletop failover and revise the estimate"], "questions": [{"id": "specialist10-2-2:q1", "prompt": "The recovery region must already serve a small live workload. What should you do?", "options": ["Use a warm standby design", "Use cold backup only", "Use an empty DNS zone as a pilot light"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Warm standby keeps a functional scaled-down environment.", "tier": 2}, {"id": "specialist10-2-2:q2", "prompt": "A design replicates every minute but restoration takes an hour. What should you do?", "options": ["Compare the one-minute recovery point and one-hour recovery time independently", "Treat RTO as the interval between replica updates", "Assume a successful backup meets both objectives"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "RPO concerns acceptable loss. RTO concerns acceptable outage.", "tier": 2}]}, {"id": "specialist11-4-1", "core": 11, "domain": 4, "objective": "4.1", "title": "Distinguish trust, encryption, hashing and governance", "type": "security", "requires": ["safety"], "lore": "Zero Trust uses explicit verification, least privilege and an assume-breach mindset. Defense in depth applies independent protections at multiple layers. Encryption provides confidentiality when keys are protected. Hashing produces a digest useful for integrity checks, but an attacker able to change the file and the published digest can defeat a naive comparison. Governance assigns decisions and accountability, risk evaluates uncertainty, and compliance assesses obligations.", "flow": ["A recipient needs to detect tampering with a download", "Compare a digest obtained from an authenticated source", "A trustworthy expected digest lets the recipient detect alteration."], "example": "A recipient needs to detect tampering with a download. Compare a digest obtained from an authenticated source. A trustworthy expected digest lets the recipient detect alteration.", "sources": [{"title": "Security, Compliance, and Identity Fundamentals official certification guide", "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/credentials/certifications/resources/study-guides/sc-900"}], "lab": ["Build a control matrix for an internal payroll app", "Assign owners to identity, network, data and audit controls", "Compare encryption, digest verification and a digital signature", "Run an assume-breach tabletop with a stolen password"], "questions": [{"id": "specialist11-4-1:q1", "prompt": "A recipient needs to detect tampering with a download. What should you do?", "options": ["Compare a digest obtained from an authenticated source", "Encrypt the file with a key published beside it and assume authenticity", "Compare filenames only"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "A trustworthy expected digest lets the recipient detect alteration.", "tier": 2}, {"id": "specialist11-4-1:q2", "prompt": "An administrator is inside the corporate network and requests privileged access. What should you do?", "options": ["Verify explicitly and grant only necessary privilege", "Grant permanent admin based on network location", "Skip logging for internal identities"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "A trusted network location does not establish identity or authorization.", "tier": 2}]}, {"id": "specialist11-1-2", "core": 11, "domain": 1, "objective": "1.2", "title": "Govern privileged and guest access with Entra", "type": "security", "requires": ["safety"], "lore": "Conditional Access evaluates signals and enforces access decisions such as requiring MFA. Privileged Identity Management supports eligible, time-bound role activation. Access reviews help reassess whether users and guests still need membership. Entitlement management organizes access packages and lifecycle rules. Microsoft Entra ID Protection detects identity risk signals, while external identities can collaborate with their own identity under applicable policies.", "flow": ["An operator needs admin privileges only during scheduled changes", "Use eligible activation with Privileged Identity Management", "Eligible activation limits the duration of elevated privileges."], "example": "An operator needs admin privileges only during scheduled changes. Use eligible activation with Privileged Identity Management. Eligible activation limits the duration of elevated privileges.", "sources": [{"title": "Security, Compliance, and Identity Fundamentals official certification guide", "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/credentials/certifications/resources/study-guides/sc-900"}], "lab": ["Create fictional operator and guest identities on paper", "Draw the approval and activation flow for privileged work", "Set the review frequency and reviewer for a guest group", "Predict access decisions for a risky and a normal sign-in"], "questions": [{"id": "specialist11-1-2:q1", "prompt": "An operator needs admin privileges only during scheduled changes. What should you do?", "options": ["Use eligible activation with Privileged Identity Management", "Give the operator a permanent global admin assignment", "Use a retention label to expire the admin role"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Eligible activation limits the duration of elevated privileges.", "tier": 2}, {"id": "specialist11-1-2:q2", "prompt": "External reviewers leave a project and group memberships may linger. What should you do?", "options": ["Schedule access reviews and act on the results", "Reset the tenant signing domain", "Turn off MFA to remove old users"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Access reviews evaluate continued entitlement.", "tier": 2}]}, {"id": "specialist11-2-2", "core": 11, "domain": 2, "objective": "2.2", "title": "Select Azure infrastructure security controls", "type": "security", "requires": ["safety"], "lore": "NSGs filter traffic at supported network scopes. Azure Firewall centralizes network filtering, and WAF evaluates supported web requests for application-layer threats. DDoS Protection addresses volumetric attacks against supported public resources. Bastion provides managed remote administration access to VMs without requiring direct public VM management ports. Key Vault protects secrets, keys and certificates, while Defender for Cloud reports posture and provides workload protection capabilities.", "flow": ["A public web application needs filtering for malicious HTTP requests", "Use a web application firewall", "WAF inspects application-layer web traffic."], "example": "A public web application needs filtering for malicious HTTP requests. Use a web application firewall. WAF inspects application-layer web traffic.", "sources": [{"title": "Security, Compliance, and Identity Fundamentals official certification guide", "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/credentials/certifications/resources/study-guides/sc-900"}], "lab": ["Draw the edge, subnet, VM and secret-store boundaries", "Match six attack or access cases to a control", "Identify which evidence shows the control actually worked", "Document one remaining risk after each mitigation"], "questions": [{"id": "specialist11-2-2:q1", "prompt": "A public web application needs filtering for malicious HTTP requests. What should you do?", "options": ["Use a web application firewall", "Use Key Vault as an HTTP request filter", "Assign a storage retention label"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "WAF inspects application-layer web traffic.", "tier": 2}, {"id": "specialist11-2-2:q2", "prompt": "A VM needs private-IP administrative access through a managed service. What should you do?", "options": ["Use Azure Bastion", "Publish RDP to all source addresses", "Use a compliance score as a tunnel"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Bastion provides an administration path without direct public VM management ports.", "tier": 2}]}, {"id": "specialist11-3-2", "core": 11, "domain": 3, "objective": "3.2", "title": "Handle audit, discovery and compliance evidence", "type": "security", "requires": ["safety"], "lore": "Service Trust Portal provides Microsoft assurance materials. Compliance Manager helps track assessment actions and progress. A compliance score reflects tracked improvements and evidence, rather than a universal compliance decision. Purview Audit records supported activity. eDiscovery supports identifying and preserving relevant material within an authorized investigation. Insider Risk Management correlates supported signals and needs careful privacy, role and workflow design.", "flow": ["An authorized investigation needs relevant records preserved and reviewed", "Use the appropriate eDiscovery case and hold workflow", "Preservation and review are discovery activities."], "example": "An authorized investigation needs relevant records preserved and reviewed. Use the appropriate eDiscovery case and hold workflow. Preservation and review are discovery activities.", "sources": [{"title": "Security, Compliance, and Identity Fundamentals official certification guide", "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/credentials/certifications/resources/study-guides/sc-900"}], "lab": ["Build a fictional case with a defined scope and custodian list", "Separate preservation, audit evidence and review actions", "Assign permissions to reviewers and log their access", "Record one compliance improvement with accountable evidence"], "questions": [{"id": "specialist11-3-2:q1", "prompt": "An authorized investigation needs relevant records preserved and reviewed. What should you do?", "options": ["Use the appropriate eDiscovery case and hold workflow", "Delete all older records before review", "Raise the compliance score and assume the content is preserved"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Preservation and review are discovery activities.", "tier": 2}, {"id": "specialist11-3-2:q2", "prompt": "A risk owner needs Microsoft cloud assurance reports. What should you do?", "options": ["Check the Service Trust Portal", "Search VM activity logs for a certification report", "Assign a sensitivity label to the subscription"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Service Trust Portal provides assurance documentation.", "tier": 2}]}, {"id": "specialist12-4-1", "core": 12, "domain": 4, "objective": "4.1", "title": "Choose a Google Cloud modernization path", "type": "software", "requires": ["safety"], "lore": "Compute Engine provides VMs and OS control. GKE provides managed Kubernetes for applications that need Kubernetes scheduling and APIs. Cloud Run runs containers without managing clusters and is useful when teams want a simpler deployment surface. Migration can rehost existing workloads or refactor them where value justifies the change. APIs, CI/CD and observability help teams deploy changes safely.", "flow": ["A team has a stateless HTTP container and wants minimal cluster administration", "Evaluate Cloud Run", "Cloud Run runs containers without requiring the team to manage a Kubernetes cluster."], "example": "A team has a stateless HTTP container and wants minimal cluster administration. Evaluate Cloud Run. Cloud Run runs containers without requiring the team to manage a Kubernetes cluster.", "sources": [{"title": "Google Cloud Digital Leader official certification guide", "url": "https://cloud.google.com/learn/certification/cloud-digital-leader"}], "lab": ["Inventory an application and its external dependencies", "Compare rehost, replatform and refactor using cost and operational effort", "Pick Compute Engine, GKE or Cloud Run for each component", "Write a rollout and rollback plan with a test transaction"], "questions": [{"id": "specialist12-4-1:q1", "prompt": "A team has a stateless HTTP container and wants minimal cluster administration. What should you do?", "options": ["Evaluate Cloud Run", "Choose GKE solely to avoid using a container", "Store the image in BigQuery and assume it will execute"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Cloud Run runs containers without requiring the team to manage a Kubernetes cluster.", "tier": 2}, {"id": "specialist12-4-1:q2", "prompt": "A legacy workload needs a custom OS dependency during an initial move. What should you do?", "options": ["Consider rehosting on Compute Engine", "Force a rewrite before any migration assessment", "Use Cloud Storage to run the OS process"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "VMs preserve more control over the runtime during rehosting.", "tier": 2}]}, {"id": "specialist12-5-1", "core": 12, "domain": 5, "objective": "5.1", "title": "Assign Google Cloud security responsibilities", "type": "software", "requires": ["safety"], "lore": "IAM grants roles to principals at scopes in the resource hierarchy. Cloud Audit Logs provide evidence about supported actions. Encryption and key management address different needs from identity and network access. Organizations still determine who should see data and verify that their application configures access appropriately. Compliance decisions need a clear data location, documented responsibilities and service-specific evidence.", "flow": ["A workload needs read access only to one bucket", "Grant a narrowly scoped reader role to the workload identity", "Least privilege limits what an identity can do and where."], "example": "A workload needs read access only to one bucket. Grant a narrowly scoped reader role to the workload identity. Least privilege limits what an identity can do and where.", "sources": [{"title": "Google Cloud Digital Leader official certification guide", "url": "https://cloud.google.com/learn/certification/cloud-digital-leader"}], "lab": ["Draw organization, folder, project and resource boundaries", "Assign a fictional workload identity a narrow role", "Define a negative access test and the audit evidence to retain", "Write a data-location requirement and its verification method"], "questions": [{"id": "specialist12-5-1:q1", "prompt": "A workload needs read access only to one bucket. What should you do?", "options": ["Grant a narrowly scoped reader role to the workload identity", "Grant organization administrator to every developer", "Store a shared permanent credential in code"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Least privilege limits what an identity can do and where.", "tier": 2}, {"id": "specialist12-5-1:q2", "prompt": "An investigator needs evidence of a supported administrative change. What should you do?", "options": ["Review the relevant Cloud Audit Logs", "Assume billing data includes every change request", "Rotate a key to reconstruct the original event"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Audit logs and billing records serve different purposes.", "tier": 2}]}, {"id": "specialist12-6-1", "core": 12, "domain": 6, "objective": "6.1", "title": "Manage Google Cloud cost and continuity", "type": "software", "requires": ["safety"], "lore": "Budgets and alerts help monitor spending but do not directly cap usage. Labels and billing exports help allocate costs. An SLI measures service behavior and an SLO sets a target. A recovery plan states the acceptable outage and data loss, who owns failover and how the restored service is validated. Sustainability discussions should distinguish regional energy characteristics from workload efficiency and resource utilization.", "flow": ["A cost alert fires after an unexpected batch workload", "Identify the cost driver and take an explicit remediation action", "An alert informs action but does not itself stop all costs."], "example": "A cost alert fires after an unexpected batch workload. Identify the cost driver and take an explicit remediation action. An alert informs action but does not itself stop all costs.", "sources": [{"title": "Google Cloud Digital Leader official certification guide", "url": "https://cloud.google.com/learn/certification/cloud-digital-leader"}], "lab": ["Define a service SLI and an error budget", "Create a fictional billing breakdown using labels", "Write a recovery runbook with RTO and RPO", "Explain which utilization changes reduce wasted capacity"], "questions": [{"id": "specialist12-6-1:q1", "prompt": "A cost alert fires after an unexpected batch workload. What should you do?", "options": ["Identify the cost driver and take an explicit remediation action", "Assume the alert stopped the job", "Disable the billing export before investigation"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "An alert informs action but does not itself stop all costs.", "tier": 2}, {"id": "specialist12-6-1:q2", "prompt": "A customer wants a target for successful requests. What should you do?", "options": ["Define an SLO using an observable success-rate SLI", "Use a backup retention period as the success-rate measure", "Treat a logo change as evidence of reliability"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "An SLO ties a target to a measurable service indicator.", "tier": 2}]}, {"id": "specialist13-4-1", "core": 13, "domain": 4, "objective": "4.1", "title": "Operate HCP Terraform workspaces and runs", "type": "software", "requires": ["safety"], "lore": "HCP Terraform workspaces organize configuration, variables, state and runs for a deployment. Projects group workspaces. Version-control-driven runs respond to changes in connected repositories, while other workflows can initiate runs through the CLI or API. Plan review, approval, and policy checks govern change. Distinguish a workspace in HCP Terraform from a CLI workspace backed by a separate state instance.", "flow": ["A team wants production changes reviewed before execution", "Require reviewed plans and approval in the production workspace workflow", "Approval gates provide a deliberate control before applying."], "example": "A team wants production changes reviewed before execution. Require reviewed plans and approval in the production workspace workflow. Approval gates provide a deliberate control before applying.", "sources": [{"title": "Terraform Associate official certification guide", "url": "https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/tutorials/certification-004/associate-review-004"}], "lab": ["Sketch dev and production workspaces with different variables", "Choose a run trigger and reviewer for each", "Predict what happens after a pull request and after merge", "Record one policy check and its evidence"], "questions": [{"id": "specialist13-4-1:q1", "prompt": "A team wants production changes reviewed before execution. What should you do?", "options": ["Require reviewed plans and approval in the production workspace workflow", "Share the production state with every repository contributor", "Turn off all run history"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Approval gates provide a deliberate control before applying.", "tier": 2}, {"id": "specialist13-4-1:q2", "prompt": "Several workspaces belong to a single product team. What should you do?", "options": ["Group them under an appropriate HCP Terraform project", "Use a provider lock file as the team permission boundary", "Merge all environments into one state file"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Projects group workspaces and support administration at that boundary.", "tier": 2}]}, {"id": "specialist13-3-2", "core": 13, "domain": 3, "objective": "3.2", "title": "Control lifecycle, validation and sensitive values", "type": "software", "requires": ["safety"], "lore": "Resource references form dependencies. Explicit depends_on can model dependencies Terraform cannot infer. create_before_destroy can reduce disruption where both resources can coexist and names and quotas permit it. Preconditions and postconditions enforce assumptions at different points. Sensitive marking hides ordinary output. Ephemeral values and write-only arguments can avoid persistence where the language and provider support them.", "flow": ["A replacement must exist before the old object disappears", "Evaluate create_before_destroy and verify naming and quota constraints", "Overlapping lifetime is useful only when both instances can exist."], "example": "A replacement must exist before the old object disappears. Evaluate create_before_destroy and verify naming and quota constraints. Overlapping lifetime is useful only when both instances can exist.", "sources": [{"title": "Terraform Associate official certification guide", "url": "https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/tutorials/certification-004/associate-review-004"}], "lab": ["Diagram a producer and dependent consumer", "Predict replacement order with and without create_before_destroy", "Write a precondition with a test case", "List which values must be kept out of plan and state"], "questions": [{"id": "specialist13-3-2:q1", "prompt": "A replacement must exist before the old object disappears. What should you do?", "options": ["Evaluate create_before_destroy and verify naming and quota constraints", "Use sensitive=true as a lifecycle setting", "Delete state before generating a plan"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Overlapping lifetime is useful only when both instances can exist.", "tier": 2}, {"id": "specialist13-3-2:q2", "prompt": "An operator wants to keep a value out of state. What should you do?", "options": ["Use supported ephemeral or write-only mechanisms where appropriate", "Mark a normal persisted output sensitive and assume it disappears", "Print the value to CI logs for verification"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Display redaction and state persistence differ.", "tier": 2}]}, {"id": "specialist13-2-2", "core": 13, "domain": 2, "objective": "2.2", "title": "Adopt existing infrastructure and reconcile drift", "type": "software", "requires": ["safety"], "lore": "Import binds an existing remote object to a resource address. Configuration must describe the intended object and the plan must be reviewed for unwanted changes. A moved block records an address change so Terraform can retain identity across a refactor. A refresh-only plan reviews external changes reflected in state without proposing ordinary configuration changes to infrastructure. Treat drift as evidence to investigate.", "flow": ["A resource changes address during a refactor but should keep the same remote object", "Use a moved block and inspect the plan", "Recording the address migration preserves identity."], "example": "A resource changes address during a refactor but should keep the same remote object. Use a moved block and inspect the plan. Recording the address migration preserves identity.", "sources": [{"title": "Terraform Associate official certification guide", "url": "https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/tutorials/certification-004/associate-review-004"}], "lab": ["Use a disposable configuration with a local provider", "Record the resource address and actual file", "Refactor the address with a moved block and inspect plan actions", "Edit the file outside Terraform and explain the detected difference"], "questions": [{"id": "specialist13-2-2:q1", "prompt": "A resource changes address during a refactor but should keep the same remote object. What should you do?", "options": ["Use a moved block and inspect the plan", "Create a duplicate at the new address and ignore the old object", "Delete remote state and apply blindly"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Recording the address migration preserves identity.", "tier": 2}, {"id": "specialist13-2-2:q2", "prompt": "An existing object should be managed without recreating it. What should you do?", "options": ["Import it at the intended address and review configuration alignment", "Rename its cloud tag and assume state discovers it", "Destroy the object and skip configuration"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Import establishes the binding. The subsequent plan reveals mismatches.", "tier": 2}]}, {"id": "specialist14-4-1", "core": 14, "domain": 4, "objective": "4.1", "title": "Manage RPM, Flatpak and shell automation", "type": "security", "requires": ["safety"], "lore": "DNF resolves RPM packages from enabled repositories. Query package ownership with rpm -qf and verify package metadata before changing repository settings. Flatpak remotes and installed apps form a separate application ecosystem. Bash scripts should quote variables, validate argument counts and report failure with a nonzero exit status. Use redirection and pipelines deliberately so expected output and diagnostic output remain distinguishable.", "flow": ["A command fails because a script argument contains spaces", "Quote the expansion when passing it to a command", "Quoting preserves the argument as a single shell word."], "example": "A command fails because a script argument contains spaces. Quote the expansion when passing it to a command. Quoting preserves the argument as a single shell word.", "sources": [{"title": "RHCSA · Red Hat System Administrator official certification guide", "url": "https://www.redhat.com/en/services/training/ex200-red-hat-certified-system-administrator-rhcsa-exam"}], "lab": ["Run dnf repolist and inspect an installed RPM with rpm -qi", "Compare the output of flatpak remotes if available", "Write a script that validates a file argument and counts matching lines", "Test a valid path, a path with spaces and a missing file"], "questions": [{"id": "specialist14-4-1:q1", "prompt": "A command fails because a script argument contains spaces. What should you do?", "options": ["Quote the expansion when passing it to a command", "Split every argument on whitespace", "Disable error handling to hide the problem"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Quoting preserves the argument as a single shell word.", "tier": 2}, {"id": "specialist14-4-1:q2", "prompt": "An RPM-managed file needs to be traced back to its package. What should you do?", "options": ["Run rpm -qf on the path", "Inspect Flatpak remotes only", "Run chmod to query package metadata"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "rpm -qf queries ownership in the RPM database.", "tier": 2}]}, {"id": "specialist14-5-1", "core": 14, "domain": 5, "objective": "5.1", "title": "Configure persistent Linux network access", "type": "security", "requires": ["safety"], "lore": "NetworkManager connection profiles describe persistent interface settings. Use nmcli to inspect the active profile and distinguish DNS, routing and link failure. firewalld applies rules through zones and distinguishes runtime from permanent configuration. SSH key authentication requires an authorized public key for the target account and correct file permissions. Test new access before closing a recovery console.", "flow": ["A firewall change works until reboot", "Inspect permanent configuration in the interface’s active zone", "Persistent firewalld changes require correct permanent zone configuration."], "example": "A firewall change works until reboot. Inspect permanent configuration in the interface’s active zone. Persistent firewalld changes require correct permanent zone configuration.", "sources": [{"title": "RHCSA · Red Hat System Administrator official certification guide", "url": "https://www.redhat.com/en/services/training/ex200-red-hat-certified-system-administrator-rhcsa-exam"}], "lab": ["Use a disposable VM and keep console access", "Record nmcli connection show, ip route and active firewall zones", "Permit a lab service in the correct zone and verify from a second isolated host", "Reboot and repeat both a permitted and denied test"], "questions": [{"id": "specialist14-5-1:q1", "prompt": "A firewall change works until reboot. What should you do?", "options": ["Inspect permanent configuration in the interface’s active zone", "Repeat only the runtime command after every reboot", "Change SSH file ownership to root for every user"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Persistent firewalld changes require correct permanent zone configuration.", "tier": 2}, {"id": "specialist14-5-1:q2", "prompt": "Name lookups fail but direct IP connections work. What should you do?", "options": ["Check resolver configuration and test the hostname separately", "Assume the network interface is down", "Rebuild the entire VM network"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Name resolution can fail while IP routing works.", "tier": 2}]}, {"id": "specialist14-6-1", "core": 14, "domain": 6, "objective": "6.1", "title": "Manage identity, sudo and SELinux context", "type": "security", "requires": ["safety"], "lore": "Local account settings include primary and supplementary groups, password aging and login shell. Use visudo to validate scoped privilege rules. SELinux type enforcement can reject access even when Unix permissions allow it. Inspect labels with ls -Z and restore expected labels with restorecon. For persistent custom labeling use appropriate policy configuration, then relabel. Investigate AVC denials before changing enforcement.", "flow": ["Files copied into a service directory have an unexpected SELinux type", "Inspect the expected context and apply restorecon where appropriate", "Mode bits and SELinux labels are different checks."], "example": "Files copied into a service directory have an unexpected SELinux type. Inspect the expected context and apply restorecon where appropriate. Mode bits and SELinux labels are different checks.", "sources": [{"title": "RHCSA · Red Hat System Administrator official certification guide", "url": "https://www.redhat.com/en/services/training/ex200-red-hat-certified-system-administrator-rhcsa-exam"}], "lab": ["Create a disposable lab user and group", "Set and inspect password aging with chage", "Validate a narrow sudoers entry with visudo", "Compare ls -Z before and after restoring a known default context"], "questions": [{"id": "specialist14-6-1:q1", "prompt": "Files copied into a service directory have an unexpected SELinux type. What should you do?", "options": ["Inspect the expected context and apply restorecon where appropriate", "Disable SELinux permanently", "Set mode 777 on every file"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Mode bits and SELinux labels are different checks.", "tier": 2}, {"id": "specialist14-6-1:q2", "prompt": "A support group needs one privileged task. What should you do?", "options": ["Use a narrowly scoped sudo rule validated with visudo", "Share the root password", "Make every member a full administrator"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Scoped elevation limits privilege to the required task.", "tier": 2}]}, {"id": "specialist14-2-2", "core": 14, "domain": 2, "objective": "2.2", "title": "Schedule jobs and verify boot behavior", "type": "security", "requires": ["safety"], "lore": "Systemd timers activate service units, cron runs recurring jobs and at schedules a one-time command. Identify the execution account and environment because an interactive shell can differ from a scheduler. Inspect default and active targets separately. journalctl can filter by boot and unit. Use chrony tooling to confirm synchronization instead of trusting that a process merely started.", "flow": ["A recurring job needs unit-based logs and activation history", "Use a systemd timer paired with a service", "A timer activates a service that can be inspected through systemd and journal logs."], "example": "A recurring job needs unit-based logs and activation history. Use a systemd timer paired with a service. A timer activates a service that can be inspected through systemd and journal logs.", "sources": [{"title": "RHCSA · Red Hat System Administrator official certification guide", "url": "https://www.redhat.com/en/services/training/ex200-red-hat-certified-system-administrator-rhcsa-exam"}], "lab": ["Write a harmless oneshot unit that appends a timestamp in a lab directory", "Create a timer and inspect it with systemctl list-timers", "Verify the unit journal and the file output", "Reboot and verify the intended startup behavior"], "questions": [{"id": "specialist14-2-2:q1", "prompt": "A recurring job needs unit-based logs and activation history. What should you do?", "options": ["Use a systemd timer paired with a service", "Use at as a perpetual scheduler without further configuration", "Start the job manually once"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "A timer activates a service that can be inspected through systemd and journal logs.", "tier": 2}, {"id": "specialist14-2-2:q2", "prompt": "A job works interactively but fails under its scheduler. What should you do?", "options": ["Inspect the execution account, environment and absolute paths", "Assume all shells supply identical configuration", "Replace every unit with a root login"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Schedulers can use different users, directories and environment variables.", "tier": 2}]}, {"id": "specialist14-3-2", "core": 14, "domain": 3, "objective": "3.2", "title": "Practice LVM, swap and network mounts", "type": "security", "requires": ["safety"], "lore": "LVM groups physical volumes into volume groups and allocates logical volumes. Growing a logical volume and growing its filesystem are distinct operations. XFS can grow while mounted but cannot be shrunk with the ordinary filesystem tools. Swap activation and persistence require separate verification. NFS mounting and autofs address remote filesystems. A timed-out remote mount can delay boot if configured carelessly.", "flow": ["An LV has grown but df still shows the previous XFS capacity", "Grow the mounted filesystem with the appropriate XFS command", "Logical capacity and filesystem capacity are separate layers."], "example": "An LV has grown but df still shows the previous XFS capacity. Grow the mounted filesystem with the appropriate XFS command. Logical capacity and filesystem capacity are separate layers.", "sources": [{"title": "RHCSA · Red Hat System Administrator official certification guide", "url": "https://www.redhat.com/en/services/training/ex200-red-hat-certified-system-administrator-rhcsa-exam"}], "lab": ["Attach a disposable lab disk and identify it by size and serial", "Create a PV, VG and LV on that disk and verify with pvs, vgs and lvs", "Mount a lab filesystem and verify source with findmnt", "Extend the LV and filesystem separately, then verify after reboot"], "questions": [{"id": "specialist14-3-2:q1", "prompt": "An LV has grown but df still shows the previous XFS capacity. What should you do?", "options": ["Grow the mounted filesystem with the appropriate XFS command", "Shrink XFS with resize2fs", "Edit only the mount-point directory name"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Logical capacity and filesystem capacity are separate layers.", "tier": 2}, {"id": "specialist14-3-2:q2", "prompt": "A network share should mount only when accessed. What should you do?", "options": ["Configure an autofs map for the share", "Mount the share on the root filesystem device directly", "Use a swap partition for NFS"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Autofs supports demand-driven mounts.", "tier": 2}]}, {"id": "specialist9-1-2", "core": 9, "domain": 1, "objective": "1.2", "title": "Administer Entra users, groups and password recovery", "type": "software", "requires": ["safety"], "lore": "Entra users may be members or external guests. Groups support repeatable access assignments and can help administer licenses, subject to service requirements. Self-service password reset requires eligible users and registered verification methods. Tenant roles govern identity tasks, while Azure resource roles govern Azure resources. Separate tenant directory administration from subscription access, and test user onboarding and removal as distinct flows.", "flow": ["A licensed user cannot use password reset", "Check whether the user is in scope and has registered the required methods", "SSPR depends on policy scope, licensing and registration."], "example": "A licensed user cannot use password reset. Check whether the user is in scope and has registered the required methods. SSPR depends on policy scope, licensing and registration.", "sources": [{"title": "Azure Administrator official certification guide", "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/credentials/certifications/resources/study-guides/az-104"}], "lab": ["Draw a user and group onboarding matrix", "Record where a license and access assignment are applied", "Model SSPR for a registered and unregistered account", "Write an offboarding verification checklist"], "questions": [{"id": "specialist9-1-2:q1", "prompt": "A licensed user cannot use password reset. What should you do?", "options": ["Check whether the user is in scope and has registered the required methods", "Assign subscription Contributor", "Disable all verification requirements"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "SSPR depends on policy scope, licensing and registration.", "tier": 2}, {"id": "specialist9-1-2:q2", "prompt": "A vendor needs temporary collaboration. What should you do?", "options": ["Invite a guest and scope access to the necessary group", "Give the vendor permanent Global Administrator", "Share an existing staff account"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Separate identities support narrower access and clearer accountability.", "tier": 2}]}, {"id": "specialist9-2-2", "core": 9, "domain": 2, "objective": "2.2", "title": "Delegate and manage Azure Storage access", "type": "software", "requires": ["safety"], "lore": "A shared access signature delegates limited access to supported storage resources. Scope permissions, target, and lifetime to the task. A stored access policy can govern supported service SAS tokens, while user delegation SAS uses Entra credentials to obtain a delegation key. Storage firewall rules remain a separate gate. Azure Files identity authentication depends on the chosen integration and has both share and file-level permission considerations. Use AzCopy or Storage Explorer to verify transfers with a test identity.", "flow": ["A contractor needs temporary read access to one blob", "Issue a scoped short-lived read SAS using a suitable authorization method", "A narrow SAS limits the permitted operation, target and duration."], "example": "A contractor needs temporary read access to one blob. Issue a scoped short-lived read SAS using a suitable authorization method. A narrow SAS limits the permitted operation, target and duration.", "sources": [{"title": "Azure Administrator official certification guide", "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/credentials/certifications/resources/study-guides/az-104"}], "lab": ["Write permissions and expiry for a mock read SAS", "Predict failures for expired, wrong-network and wrong-operation requests", "Compare a service SAS and a user delegation SAS", "Plan an AzCopy transfer and checksum verification using test data"], "questions": [{"id": "specialist9-2-2:q1", "prompt": "A contractor needs temporary read access to one blob. What should you do?", "options": ["Issue a scoped short-lived read SAS using a suitable authorization method", "Hand over the account key", "Make all containers public"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "A narrow SAS limits the permitted operation, target and duration.", "tier": 2}, {"id": "specialist9-2-2:q2", "prompt": "A valid storage credential is denied from an unapproved network. What should you do?", "options": ["Check storage networking independently of authorization", "Increase the SAS expiry to bypass the firewall", "Change every blob to Archive"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "A credential does not override network access rules.", "tier": 2}]}, {"id": "specialist9-3-2", "core": 9, "domain": 3, "objective": "3.2", "title": "Route and balance Azure network traffic", "type": "software", "requires": ["safety"], "lore": "Peering connects compatible virtual networks, but it is not transitive by default. A route table can send selected prefixes through a network virtual appliance when forwarding and return paths are correctly designed. Azure Load Balancer uses layer-four flows and health probes. A private endpoint relies on correct name resolution to direct clients to its private IP. Use effective routes, effective rules and connectivity diagnostics before changing a working network.", "flow": ["Two spokes peer only with a hub and cannot reach one another", "Design explicit routing through the hub or an appropriate direct peer", "Spoke-to-spoke reachability requires an intended forwarding path."], "example": "Two spokes peer only with a hub and cannot reach one another. Design explicit routing through the hub or an appropriate direct peer. Spoke-to-spoke reachability requires an intended forwarding path.", "sources": [{"title": "Azure Administrator official certification guide", "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/credentials/certifications/resources/study-guides/az-104"}], "lab": ["Sketch a hub and two spoke networks with nonoverlapping prefixes", "Predict effective routes for one packet in each direction", "Trace a private endpoint name resolution path", "Write a load balancer probe failure and verification checklist"], "questions": [{"id": "specialist9-3-2:q1", "prompt": "Two spokes peer only with a hub and cannot reach one another. What should you do?", "options": ["Design explicit routing through the hub or an appropriate direct peer", "Assume peering automatically creates transitive routes", "Change the VM size to fix route selection"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Spoke-to-spoke reachability requires an intended forwarding path.", "tier": 2}, {"id": "specialist9-3-2:q2", "prompt": "A healthy application is removed from a load balancer backend. What should you do?", "options": ["Verify that the configured health probe can reach its expected port and endpoint", "Publish a new DNS name before inspecting health", "Add more storage replicas"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Backend health depends on the configured probe, not solely on the process state.", "tier": 2}]}, {"id": "specialist10-3-2", "core": 10, "domain": 3, "objective": "3.2", "title": "Optimize AWS spend by workload behavior", "type": "software", "requires": ["safety"], "lore": "Compute pricing models trade predictability and flexibility. On-Demand avoids a long commitment. Savings Plans exchange a usage commitment for eligible discounts. Spot capacity can be interrupted and is appropriate for workloads that tolerate it. S3 lifecycle decisions depend on object size, age, access frequency and retrieval requirements. Network paths can incur data processing and transfer charges. Use observed utilization and cost allocation before purchasing commitments.", "flow": ["A fault-tolerant job can checkpoint and retry interrupted workers", "Evaluate Spot for the flexible workers", "Interruptible compute can reduce cost when workloads recover from interruption."], "example": "A fault-tolerant job can checkpoint and retry interrupted workers. Evaluate Spot for the flexible workers. Interruptible compute can reduce cost when workloads recover from interruption.", "sources": [{"title": "AWS Solutions Architect Associate official certification guide", "url": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/aws-certification/latest/solutions-architect-associate-03.html"}], "lab": ["Split a fictional workload into baseline and burst capacity", "Model the impact of worker interruption and checkpointing", "List retrieval and transfer costs for the data flow", "Record one rightsizing change and the measurement that supports it"], "questions": [{"id": "specialist10-3-2:q1", "prompt": "A fault-tolerant job can checkpoint and retry interrupted workers. What should you do?", "options": ["Evaluate Spot for the flexible workers", "Purchase a long commitment before measuring the load", "Move the job to an archival storage class"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Interruptible compute can reduce cost when workloads recover from interruption.", "tier": 2}, {"id": "specialist10-3-2:q2", "prompt": "A stable baseline runs every day and the team has measured expected usage. What should you do?", "options": ["Evaluate a suitable commitment while retaining flexibility for bursts", "Put every mission-critical instance on interruptible capacity", "Assume a budget notification discounts the bill"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Commitments are most useful against predictable eligible usage.", "tier": 2}]}, {"id": "specialist12-2-2", "core": 12, "domain": 2, "objective": "2.2", "title": "Design a Google Cloud analytics pipeline", "type": "software", "requires": ["safety"], "lore": "Cloud Storage holds objects, BigQuery provides analytical SQL, and Cloud SQL serves relational transactional use cases. Pub/Sub decouples event producers and consumers, while Dataflow runs stream or batch processing pipelines. Data governance includes metadata, access boundaries, quality and lineage. A pipeline needs explicit handling for late data and duplicated events. Test with synthetic records before using customer data.", "flow": ["A team needs analytical SQL over large event history", "Evaluate BigQuery for the analytical workload", "The warehouse is suited to large-scale analytics."], "example": "A team needs analytical SQL over large event history. Evaluate BigQuery for the analytical workload. The warehouse is suited to large-scale analytics.", "sources": [{"title": "Google Cloud Digital Leader official certification guide", "url": "https://cloud.google.com/learn/certification/cloud-digital-leader"}], "lab": ["Draw an event flow from producers through messaging and processing to analytics", "Define an event ID and a deduplication rule", "Specify where schema validation and access controls apply", "Test late and duplicated synthetic events on paper"], "questions": [{"id": "specialist12-2-2:q1", "prompt": "A team needs analytical SQL over large event history. What should you do?", "options": ["Evaluate BigQuery for the analytical workload", "Use an application load balancer to aggregate rows", "Use a DNS zone as the storage layer"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "The warehouse is suited to large-scale analytics.", "tier": 2}, {"id": "specialist12-2-2:q2", "prompt": "Producers must send events independently of processing availability. What should you do?", "options": ["Use Pub/Sub with a consumer that can handle redelivery", "Write all events to a VM boot disk and require synchronous reads", "Remove duplicate handling to increase throughput"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Messaging decouples producers and consumers, while redelivery needs deliberate handling.", "tier": 2}]}, {"id": "specialist14-1-2", "core": 14, "domain": 1, "objective": "1.2", "title": "Work with Linux archives, links and text filters", "type": "security", "requires": ["safety"], "lore": "Hard links name the same inode and generally cannot cross filesystems. Symbolic links reference paths and can dangle if the target moves. Use tar to bundle files and verify archive contents before extraction. grep filters by patterns, and pipelines carry command output to another process. Shell redirection handles standard output and standard error separately. Use local manual pages to check options and practice in a disposable directory.", "flow": ["A pathname should reference a target on another filesystem", "Create a symbolic link", "A symbolic link stores a pathname rather than sharing an inode across filesystems."], "example": "A pathname should reference a target on another filesystem. Create a symbolic link. A symbolic link stores a pathname rather than sharing an inode across filesystems.", "sources": [{"title": "RHCSA · Red Hat System Administrator official certification guide", "url": "https://www.redhat.com/en/services/training/ex200-red-hat-certified-system-administrator-rhcsa-exam"}], "lab": ["Create a harmless file plus hard and symbolic links", "Compare inode numbers using ls -li and observe what happens after renaming the original", "Archive only the lab files and inspect tar output before extracting into a new directory", "Use grep and redirection to capture matching lines and errors separately"], "questions": [{"id": "specialist14-1-2:q1", "prompt": "A pathname should reference a target on another filesystem. What should you do?", "options": ["Create a symbolic link", "Create a hard link across the filesystem boundary", "Copy the file and assume edits stay linked"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "A symbolic link stores a pathname rather than sharing an inode across filesystems.", "tier": 2}, {"id": "specialist14-1-2:q2", "prompt": "A script must keep normal output and diagnostics in separate files. What should you do?", "options": ["Redirect stdout and stderr separately", "Use only a pipe and assume stderr follows stdout", "Overwrite the source file with both streams"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "File descriptor 1 is standard output and 2 is standard error.", "tier": 2}]}, {"id": "specialist14-2-3", "core": 14, "domain": 2, "objective": "2.3", "title": "Investigate processes and system boot", "type": "security", "requires": ["safety"], "lore": "Use ps and top to identify CPU or memory consumers and check ownership before acting. SIGTERM allows a process to handle orderly shutdown, while SIGKILL cannot be caught. Nice values influence ordinary process scheduling priority. Use journalctl with a boot or unit filter to scope evidence. Boot target selection and bootloader changes require a recovery path. Keep a VM snapshot before experimenting with startup recovery.", "flow": ["A background task must stop and can clean up on exit", "Send SIGTERM first and verify termination", "A graceful signal gives a cooperative process a chance to release resources."], "example": "A background task must stop and can clean up on exit. Send SIGTERM first and verify termination. A graceful signal gives a cooperative process a chance to release resources.", "sources": [{"title": "RHCSA · Red Hat System Administrator official certification guide", "url": "https://www.redhat.com/en/services/training/ex200-red-hat-certified-system-administrator-rhcsa-exam"}], "lab": ["Launch a harmless sleep process and identify its PID and parent", "Inspect priority and send SIGTERM to that specific lab process", "Compare current and previous boot records in a disposable VM", "Record the default systemd target and write a recovery plan"], "questions": [{"id": "specialist14-2-3:q1", "prompt": "A background task must stop and can clean up on exit. What should you do?", "options": ["Send SIGTERM first and verify termination", "Send SIGKILL to every process owned by the user", "Reboot the whole host without inspecting the process"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "A graceful signal gives a cooperative process a chance to release resources.", "tier": 2}, {"id": "specialist14-2-3:q2", "prompt": "An error occurred during the prior boot. What should you do?", "options": ["Query the previous boot journal if it is retained", "Search only the live process list", "Clear persistent logs first"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Boot filtering isolates records from the relevant boot.", "tier": 2}]}];
